and (B) we got 10/x = 2y/5 => 2xy = 50 => xy=25. Good.\n\nThus answer: \\boxed{25}.</think>The given equations are \\(\\log_x(y^x) = 10\\) and \\(\\log_y(x^{4y}) = 10\\). Rewriting in exponential form:\n\n\\[\nx^{10} = y^x \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad y^{10} = x^{4y}.\n\\]\n\nTake natural logarithms:\n\n\\[\n10 \\ln x = x \\ln y \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\ln y = \\frac{10 \\ln x}{x},\n\\]\n\\[\n10 \\ln y = 4y \\ln x \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\ln y = \\frac{4y \\ln x}{10} = \\frac{2y \\ln x}{5}.\n\\]\n\nEquating the expressions for \\(\\ln y\\) (since \\(\\ln x > 0\\) for \\(x>1\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10 \\ln x}{x} = \\frac{2y \\ln x}{5} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{10}{x} = \\frac{2y}{5}.\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying gives \\(10 \\cdot 5 = 2y \\cdot x\\), i.e., \\(50 = 2xy\\), so \\(xy = 25\\).\n\nThus the value of \\(xy\\) is \\(\\boxed{25}\\).<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>"] reward={'score': 1.0, 'acc': True, 'pred': '25'}
